+---
+id: dl-009-createpatch-describes-only-what-changed-as-a-minimal-rfc-6902-patch
+type: decision-log
+title: "createPatch describes only what changed, as a minimal RFC 6902 patch"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Clients want to send only what changed between two versions of a JSON document. A patch that repeats the
+whole new document defeats that purpose, so `createPatch(from, to)` must describe the changes and nothing
+more. RFC 6902 gives the operations to do this: `add`, `remove` and `replace` at a JSON Pointer, applied in
+order. Its `replace` at the empty pointer `""` replaces the whole document, which `applyPatch` supports
+(dl-005).
+
+## Decision
+
+- `createPatch` compares `from` and `to` recursively, starting at the root with the empty pointer:
+  - two equal values give no operation;
+  - two objects are compared member by member: a member only in `from` is removed, a member only in `to`
+    is added, and a member in both is compared recursively at its own pointer;
+  - two arrays are aligned by their elements: the longest common subsequence of equal elements is kept, the
+    elements between two kept elements are paired by position and compared recursively, and the extra
+    elements are removed or added at their current index;
+  - any other pair of values gives a single `replace` at the pointer of the value, so a root whose type
+    changes gives one `replace` at `""`.
+- Operations are emitted in the order they are applied, so each index is valid when its operation runs:
+  the array indices are tracked as the earlier operations of the same array are emitted.
+- `move` and `copy` are not detected: a value that moves is removed at one place and added at another.
+- To keep the cost bounded, the array alignment uses the common subsequence only while the number of
+  element pairs in the unaligned part stays below a fixed limit. Above it, elements are paired by position
+  only. The patch stays correct but can be larger.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Changing one member of an object, or one element of an array, gives one operation at that member's
+  pointer, not the whole document.
+- A patch produced by `createPatch` applied with `applyPatch` gives a result equal to `to`. Tests check this
+  round trip.
+- The alignment costs time and memory proportional to the product of the lengths of the unaligned parts of
+  two arrays, which is why the limit exists. Very large arrays that change in many places may produce
+  longer patches than the minimum.
