@@ -0,0 +1,31 @@
+---
+id: dl-010-createpatch-never-mutates-its-inputs-and-does-not-throw-for-json-values
+type: decision-log
+title: "createPatch never mutates its inputs and does not throw for JSON values"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+## Context
+
+`from` and `to` are often shared with other code that still reads them (dl-003, dl-008). A patch is a value
+that callers keep and send, so it must not share objects with the documents it was made from: a later change
+to one would otherwise change the other.
+
+## Decision
+
+- `createPatch(from, to)` never modifies `from` or `to`. It only reads them.
+- Every `value` in the returned patch is a copy made with `structuredClone`, so the patch shares no object
+  with `from` or `to`, as `applyPatch` copies the values it takes from a patch (dl-003).
+- `createPatch` does not throw for two JSON values: every pair of JSON values has a patch. Values that are not
+  JSON (for example a `Date` or a function) are compared and copied as they are, and `structuredClone` throws
+  for a function, as it does in `applyPatch` and `applyMergePatch`. Whether such input should be rejected is
+  left to dl-006.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The caller can rely on `from` and `to` being unchanged, and can change the returned patch without affecting
+  either document.
+- Copying the values costs memory proportional to the size of the changed parts of the documents, not to the
+  whole document, because only the values in the patch are copied.
+- A caller does not need a `try`/`catch` for JSON input.
